1
00:00:01,309 --> 00:00:05,349
Have you ever tried to debug a deployment only to find your

2
00:00:05,349 --> 00:00:07,710
container image is sitting somewhere else?

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,510
It might be in someone's local Docker registry or might be

4
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:13,630
stored in a deprecated Container Registry.

5
00:00:13,789 --> 00:00:18,040
Artifact management can make or break your entire CI/CD pipeline.

6
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:21,350
That's why Google Cloud's Artifact Registry is

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:23,632
designed to be a single source of truth.

8
00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:26,462
Now let's see how we can use it for better.

9
00:00:26,702 --> 00:00:30,603
First of all, Artifact Registry supports multiple formats.

10
00:00:30,702 --> 00:00:37,042
It supports Docker, Maven, NPM, Python, Go, and generic OCI.

11
00:00:37,193 --> 00:00:42,062
Now for this, I've just created one application that is Docker‑based.

12
00:00:42,222 --> 00:00:45,576
So if I go down to my Open editor, there I have a

13
00:00:45,576 --> 00:00:48,855
sample Flask app that we have created.

14
00:00:49,055 --> 00:00:54,055
Now for this, I'm going to create a repository which is of the format of Docker.

15
00:00:54,236 --> 00:00:56,675
So here it is just saying Hello world.

16
00:00:56,816 --> 00:00:58,296
Now to create the repository,

17
00:00:58,416 --> 00:01:01,096
I go back to my terminal window, and I'm going to type

18
00:01:01,096 --> 00:01:04,275
command like gcloud artifacts repositories.

19
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:10,269
And then I'm saying create my‑flask‑app‑repo. That is the name of my repository.

20
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:16,430
Then I'm giving the repository format as Docker and location as asia‑southeast1,

21
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:21,220
and then any description, whatever you like to give and then say Enter.

22
00:01:21,279 --> 00:01:25,629
This will take a while, and it is going to create our artifact repository.

23
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:26,610
Not only this,

24
00:01:26,870 --> 00:01:31,959
if you have noticed that we have specifically mentioned the region location.

25
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:36,510
Google recommends placing artifact repository in the same region

26
00:01:36,510 --> 00:01:40,230
as your GKE cluster or your Cloud Run service,

27
00:01:40,349 --> 00:01:43,330
and it also recommends that create the artifact repository in

28
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:46,273
the same region where Cloud Build executes.

29
00:01:46,313 --> 00:01:48,722
Actually, this reduces the network latency,

30
00:01:48,803 --> 00:01:51,962
egress cost, build time, and deploy time.

31
00:01:52,252 --> 00:01:55,303
Maybe, let's say for enterprise deployment, you

32
00:01:55,483 --> 00:01:57,483
wanted to do across multiple regions.

33
00:01:57,602 --> 00:02:02,822
In that case, you can create the multiple repos for each and every region,

34
00:02:02,843 --> 00:02:06,742
and then you can use the replication or the promotion pipelines.

35
00:02:06,763 --> 00:02:08,502
If you want to see how this looks like,

36
00:02:08,602 --> 00:02:12,136
you can come down here and search for Artifact Registry.

37
00:02:12,335 --> 00:02:15,776
Now here you can see all the registry which is there in your account.

38
00:02:15,936 --> 00:02:19,830
So here you can see that all the registry which is there in my account,

39
00:02:19,830 --> 00:02:23,335
what I can do is I can go down to my specific repository.

40
00:02:23,455 --> 00:02:26,365
I can specifically assign the IAM roles,

41
00:02:26,496 --> 00:02:29,776
which sometimes is very important based on the

42
00:02:29,776 --> 00:02:32,136
specific operation someone wanted to do.

43
00:02:32,296 --> 00:02:38,110
So I can select my application and say Show Info panel. And here I

44
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:40,470
can add these specific roles and permission.

45
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:44,720
I can say Add principal, and for a specific set of users or a group,

46
00:02:45,809 --> 00:02:47,169
I can assign the roles.

47
00:02:47,919 --> 00:02:51,210
Maybe I'm interested in assigning the Artifact Reader Role,

48
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:55,139
or maybe I'm interested in assigning the writer permission for,

49
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:59,270
let's say, Cloud Build, or maybe read permission for Cloud Deploy.

50
00:02:59,369 --> 00:03:00,990
So according to the need,

51
00:03:01,089 --> 00:03:05,009
we can assign the roles and permission for this specific registry.

52
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:08,880
Enterprises use the Artifact Registry for a couple of more reasons.

53
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:10,992
One of them is a vulnerability scanning.

54
00:03:11,091 --> 00:03:16,212
Google automatically runs and scans the vulnerabilities on pushed images.

55
00:03:16,292 --> 00:03:20,412
You can enforce the policies to block the deployments when critical

56
00:03:20,412 --> 00:03:25,371
CVs found fail builds if base images are outdated.

57
00:03:25,481 --> 00:03:29,531
Another important best practice is to have your immutable tagging.

58
00:03:29,651 --> 00:03:36,011
You never overwrite the tags like prod or let us use the commit SHAs

59
00:03:36,011 --> 00:03:39,031
instead and then promote the version's metadata,

60
00:03:39,531 --> 00:03:40,634
not re‑tags.

61
00:03:40,813 --> 00:03:45,494
By default, the Artifact Registry is private, which is exactly what you want.

62
00:03:45,694 --> 00:03:47,194
With the binary authorization,

63
00:03:47,494 --> 00:03:51,274
teams can sign container images before they deploy.

64
00:03:51,414 --> 00:03:56,123
This prevents tampered images, unapproved builds, unauthorized pushes.

65
00:03:56,373 --> 00:04:01,154
Specifically, many finance and healthcare organizations require this.

66
00:04:01,333 --> 00:04:03,274
Not only this, over a period of time,

67
00:04:03,494 --> 00:04:06,494
the artifacts build up hundreds of the versions,

68
00:04:06,654 --> 00:04:08,414
old images, unused tags,

69
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:12,296
all taking up this space and money. Artifact Registry

70
00:04:12,296 --> 00:04:14,975
supports the cleanup policy that you can set.

71
00:04:15,186 --> 00:04:18,365
For example, you can go back to your repository.

72
00:04:18,454 --> 00:04:21,815
When you go inside your repository and say Edit repository,

73
00:04:21,915 --> 00:04:26,075
in your repository here itself, you can add the cleanup policy.

74
00:04:26,135 --> 00:04:27,885
And when you set up a cleanup policy,

75
00:04:28,055 --> 00:04:31,496
you can give a name and you can decide the basis on

76
00:04:31,496 --> 00:04:33,055
which you want to do cleanup.

77
00:04:33,335 --> 00:04:35,656
Maybe you wanted to do the conditional delete,

78
00:04:35,816 --> 00:04:40,697
saying that if a specific image is older then this is specific,

79
00:04:40,898 --> 00:04:41,817
then you can remove it.

80
00:04:41,898 --> 00:04:46,537
Or maybe you wanted to keep only some specific number of recent version.

81
00:04:46,768 --> 00:04:49,157
Say, I don't want it to have more than 50 images,

82
00:04:49,257 --> 00:04:51,778
so you can keep that specific number of accounts.

83
00:04:51,937 --> 00:04:55,097
So this is how you can even set up your cleanup policy.

84
00:04:55,227 --> 00:04:59,578
This is going to be extremely useful for the teams doing the frequent releases.

85
00:04:59,697 --> 00:05:03,738
So that's how you can configure the Artifact Registry for a secure,

86
00:05:03,977 --> 00:05:08,898
scalable CI/CD workflow and integration with the Cloud Build and Cloud Deploy.
